config: saas.txt — the third case, and the row budget it deliberately does not spend

## What this adds

`sites.txt` records names. `san-list.txt` records the certificate those names need. Neither has a
place for **somebody else's domain pointed into one of our names**, which is what a trade node is for
and what `discoverywritten.com` is about to be. `config/saas.txt` is that record.

## The property worth noticing

**A consumer's certificate is not a SAN row.** Cloudflare for SaaS issues one certificate per custom
hostname, so the advanced-certificate pack — capped at 50 hosts, aborting rather than truncating —
does not grow by a row per consumer. The cost model `san-list.txt` reasons so carefully about applies
to our own name space only. The fallback origin needs no row either; `*.anecdote.channel` covers it.

## The state it records honestly

- **Fallback origin** `fallback.anecdote.channel`, `pending` — one dashboard action, not in this repo.
- **`www.discoverywritten.com` first, apex second.** The apex is an active zone in the same account
  serving its own content, and a custom hostname for a name Cloudflare is already authoritative for
  is the one untested part. `www` does not resolve at all (measured), so it is a free subject: prove
  the mechanism where there is no traffic to break, then decide about the apex.
- **Nothing for FCPM.** Different account; holding both is not a shortcut.

Also: a note under the `discoverywritten.trade…` entry in `sites.txt` recording why `repo:` is still
correct (the name has no DNS yet) and what drops it.

## Merge qualifications

- [x] no SAN change needed — `*.trade.fort-collins.colorado.anecdote.channel` already covers the
      service name, `*.anecdote.channel` covers the fallback origin
- [x] declaration only; nothing here provisions anything
- [ ] the dashboard actions, which are the operator's
